<p>
  If you have seen a lizard in Miami, it was probably a brown anole. The species
  comes from Cuba and the Bahamas and has spread across the entire state of
  Florida and far beyond it. In 2018 we reported the first brown anoles on
  Ascension Island, in the middle of the Atlantic, and on Bermuda its arrival
  poses a new threat to the critically endangered Bermuda skink.
</p>
<p>
  Brown anoles love the sun and are among the few anoles active through the
  hottest part of the day. They are also the champion displayers of the group.
  Watch any tree trunk for a few minutes and a male will fan his dewlap, bob his
  head and do push-ups, a message to other males that this trunk is taken.
</p>

<h2>City lizards</h2>
<p>
  Brown anoles thrive in cities, and part of the reason is behavioral. When we
  compared males in urban and natural forest habitats in Miami, urban lizards
  perched on broader surfaces, used walls and other human-made structures, jumped
  less and changed perches less often. They also displayed their dewlaps more than
  twice as often. Urban sites were more open, with roughly a third of the tree
  density, so a displaying lizard can be seen from further away, which may explain
  the jump in signaling.
</p>

<h2>Losing ground to a newcomer</h2>
<p>
  On Lizard Island, our long-term study site in Miami, brown anoles made up 75%
  of all adult anoles in October 2017. Then the Puerto Rican crested anole arrived.
  Four years later brown anoles were just 18% of the community, and the number of
  adult males had fallen from 168 to 54. Where the two species live together across
  Miami, brown anoles perch lower and spend more time on the ground, and on Lizard
  Island natural selection began favoring brown anoles with relatively longer hind
  limbs, the build that suits running on broad, open surfaces. The full story is on
  our <a href="/research/lizard-island/">Lizard Island page</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Brown anoles also appear in another story from the lab: a
  three-legged brown anole in the Bahamas started the project described on our
  <a href="/research/three-legged-lizards/">three-legged lizards page</a>.
</p>
